---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="Project | Trajectory CoPilot" description="Project overview for Trajectory CoPilot: an agentic interface for astrodynamics and mission design.">
  <section class="space-y-8">
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <p class="text-sm uppercase tracking-[0.3em] text-orbital">Project overview</p>
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold">Trajectory CoPilot</h1>
      <p class="max-w-4xl text-slate-300">An agentic interface for astrodynamics and mission design that helps engineers explore, validate, and iterate on trajectory solutions while keeping the results grounded in physics-based verification.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-2">
      <div class="card space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Problem statement</h2>
        <p class="text-slate-300">Trajectory design is becoming increasingly complex as more spacecraft operate in cislunar space and encounter three-body dynamics, uncertain environments, and changing mission objectives. Traditional trajectory work demands significant expertise and time, which limits how quickly engineers can explore alternatives.</p>
        <p class="text-slate-300">This project asks whether an AI agent can act as a trajectory copilot that helps with design exploration while still producing physically correct, verifiable answers.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Why it matters</h2>
        <p class="text-slate-300">For space operations, especially in the Space Force context, fast and informed decisions matter when assets face known and unknown threats. AI systems may help planners explore more alternatives faster, but they must remain grounded in physics-based validation.</p>
        <p class="text-slate-300">I want to understand how an LLM can serve as an interface between mission planners and existing analysis tools such as GMAT, while still maintaining engineering rigor.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-2">
      <div class="card space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Research question</h2>
        <p class="text-slate-300">Can an AI agent reliably assist with trajectory design while using established analysis tools to verify its results?</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Methodology</h2>
        <p class="text-slate-300">I will start with two-body astrodynamics problems and progress to Clohessy-Wiltshire rendezvous and one-orbit, two-burn rendezvous cases. I want to map the relationship between transfer duration and required delta-V, then test when assumptions break down.</p>
        <p class="text-slate-300">GMAT will serve as an independent verification environment, alongside other analytical or Python-based checks, so the agent can be evaluated on both correctness and usefulness.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Workflow</h2>
      <div class="mt-6 flex flex-col items-center gap-4 md:flex-row md:justify-between">
        <div class="flow-node pulse">Problem</div>
        <div class="text-orbital">→</div>
        <div class="flow-node pulse">Agent</div>
        <div class="text-orbital">→</div>
        <div class="flow-node pulse">Verify</div>
        <div class="text-orbital">→</div>
        <div class="flow-node pulse">Iterate</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-2">
      <div class="card space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Planned phases</h2>
        <ol class="space-y-3 text-slate-300">
          <li><strong>Phase 1:</strong> Solve benchmark problems from Professor Miller's previous orbital dynamics material.</li>
          <li><strong>Phase 2:</strong> Use GMAT and publicly available mission scenarios to verify the agent’s outputs.</li>
          <li><strong>Phase 3:</strong> Test harder rendezvous and cislunar cases to see when assumptions fail.</li>
          <li><strong>Phase 4:</strong> Identify what information and prompting structure are needed for reliable solutions.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <div class="card space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Success criteria</h2>
        <ul class="space-y-3 text-slate-300">
          <li>Correct mathematical setup of trajectory problems</li>
          <li>Reliable detection of when assumptions no longer apply</li>
          <li>Successful physics-based verification in GMAT</li>
          <li>Clear identification of missing information or constraints</li>
          <li>Improved efficiency in exploring trajectory design options</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-2">
      <div class="card space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Data and restrictions</h2>
        <p class="text-slate-300">The first phase uses MIT orbital dynamics class material that is not data restricted. The second phase uses GMAT and publicly available examples and mission scenarios. No proprietary, classified, or otherwise restricted data will be used.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Long-term vision</h2>
        <p class="text-slate-300">If successful, this project can form the basis for future research into AI-assisted mission planning in increasingly complex orbital environments, including cislunar operations and other dynamic mission design problems.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
